welcome back to the show couple  education stories I want to cover we  don't always get the opportunity to talk  about education and I think maybe  because I've been doing some teaching  myself I am more and more sort of in the  world of paying attention to what's  going on with with education and  different aspects in different ways in  our country and there is a story out of  New Mexico that I wanted to tell you  about New Mexico proposed some new  science curriculum standards and they  were a complete and utter abomination  they included the deletion and omission  of global warming references the removal  of evolution references from science  class the removal of references to the  age of the earth according to science  which hint is not 6,000 years old but is  4.54 billion years old and as a result  of this and and this is a good thing you  would not necessarily expect that this  would happen because hey people have  lives and people are busy suggestions  from the public came in as they are  being called and I think a better way to  describe them would be angry demands  that you put that stuff back into the  science curriculum and it is indeed  going to happen the age of the earth as  science knows it he's going to be riad  'add to the new mexico science  curriculum the reality of the rise in  global temperatures over the last  century which is a component in climate  change is going to be added back in to  the new mexico science curriculum the  process of evolution is going to be  added back in so this is great news but  why were they removed in the first place  the the new revelation is that this  stuff is going to be put back in but a  better question would be why was it  removed in the first place and sadly  this has been happening for years across  the country there have been these pushes  to take evolution out of school and this  is you know sometimes just as insidious  is the demand that intelligent design or  creationism be taught alongside  evolution in science class and I've said  this before and I am very steadfast in  this belief I've really been covering  this issue for some time if you want to  teach intelligent design in religion  class have added right that is a  religious belief and it has its place in  religion class I would also say if it's  a general religion class you should  explain or teach creation stories from  other religions as well if it's a class  on Christianity then that is a perfect  place to explain that hey the view of  Christianity on creation is that there  was intelligent design or creationism or  some combination of the two but not in  science class and particularly not in  science class  as the one other story that you would  teach alongside evolution of how things  came to be the way they were even that  even the idea of saying in science class  we will teach evolution and we will  teach Christian creationism or  intelligent design once you add any  religious belief into science class  which i think is wrong you really should  be teaching the beliefs of I wouldn't  say all because there are hundreds or  even thousands but at least of several  religions on the creation of our planet  of our universe of the earth so this is  this is happening in the United States  it's not just an American phenomenon I  told you some months ago that in Turkey  schools are going to or probably at this  point did I believe it was starting this  past fall if I recall this fall this  current semester that we're in schools  in Turkey going to stop teaching  evolution endless public schools that  are actually teaching creationism in  science class Louisiana and Tennessee  probably the greatest offenders when it  comes to creationism and intelligent  design in science class and in January I  think it was within the first maybe the  first week after the inauguration of  Donald Trump a number of anti evolution  in education bills that were passed or  that were proposed rather so this is  having a real impact and I always you  know one thing I'm thinking about is we  know that beating right-wingers over the  head with facts is really not going to  bring them over to our side so how can  we talk to right-wingers in a way that  we might be able to appeal to their  sensibilities maybe when it comes to  education and math and science and not  going the religious way we should be  saying hey this is an issue of  patriotism right if we fall behind other  countries in math and science we are not  going to be able to compete and we're no  longer gonna be the best or something  like that or maybe it's national  security math and science Direct relate  directly to the development of defense  programs the development of I don't know  what else and we don't want to fall  behind other countries in terms of our  national security capabilities I mean  again these are not arguments that  particularly resonate with me but I'm  thinking of how we might be able to  appeal to the values of the right to  really explain why you've got to get  this stuff out and the Christian  homeschooling trend is part of the  problem because students are showing up  at colleges if they don't go to  Christian colleges you have students  that are being raised within the world  of Christian homeschooling and then they  show up in college where there's I  fortunately at most colleges not really  a place for creationism and intelligent  design in science classes and they're  unprepared so maybe if we appeal to the  right along the lines of patriotism and  national security we could try to make  some inroads but I am not particularly  optimistic because the trend is towards  more of this nonsense not towards less  of it

